From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:33:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] feat(Detective #123): the effect family's raise-inject,
 state-extra and state-noop's realization are pinned by intent tests

The defect, verbatim. "`raise`, the inject half, is not built. The catalog marks `raise` `partial`. Its
reason: an injected raise fails every test that reaches the call, so its kill measures reach, not
specification." "`state-noop` has no OUTPUT sub-mode ... No ruling is recorded on whether the positive
realization meets it." "`state-extra` is not generated. The catalog marks it `unsupported`: the function
determines no object or attribute to mutate."

Why the obvious fix is wrong. Injecting an arbitrary exception is a guess, and one that cannot be built
(a local class, a type that needs two constructor arguments, an interrupt) would be a different failure
than the one the mode names. And an extra write to an argument the function writes on purpose is not an
unintended side effect. Both are facts the baseline observes (38bec04): the exception types that LEFT the
function (else those its own body raises, resolved against its globals and the builtins), and each
parameter's structural state before and after every call.

What each state means. raise_inject@<exception> raises at the return point, after the body ran; its kill
is `reach_class` (REACH_CLASS_MODES, `kill_class_of_dimension`), which the census lane's detection/contract
split reads off the mutant's dimension; it is recorded in the catalog as measuring reach, not
specification. `raise_standing` (pinned) names why an observed raise is not injected: local_class,
base_exception (the engine's interrupts derive from BaseException; nothing injects one), unresolvable,
not_constructible; a function that raises nothing observed or written is named
no_exception_type_observed_or_raised; a raise of a bound name is no type. state_extra@<parameter> writes one
extra entry (a dict key, list or deque element, set member, attribute) to an argument or the receiver
observed mutable in EVERY call and mutated in none; `extra_write_standing` (pinned) names why not:
calls_truncated, mutated, type_varies, and a function with no mutable argument is named too. State-noop is
the positive STATE:remove_assign and STMT deletion (decision D2, Rem. 11.9(b)): no new sub-mode, recorded
in the catalog and on `MutationPolicy.categories["OUTPUT"]["realized_by"]`.

What was NOT closed. The wrapper that cannot build its exception or bind its argument returns as the
function does (a no-op, never a different failure). A generator takes neither mode (its output is its
yields). The extra write persists in the test's own argument object; evaluation gives each test fresh
arguments (W#55). Detective's harvest still carries type names (see 38bec04), so a Detective `--two-sign`
run does not yet generate these.

Sensitivity: with the parameterized Form B modes withheld 7 of the 46 intent tests fail.
Pins: `raise_standing`, `extra_write_standing`, `kill_class_of_dimension` (see the pin receipts in the
report; the receipts for these three are recorded after this commit).

Tests: tests/test_mu_minus_effect_premise_intent.py 46 passed; with the catalog, doc, policy and
instrumentation-ownership guards 117 passed under Python 3.14 and 117 under 3.12, PYTHONUNBUFFERED unset.
ruff clean under pinned 0.16.7.
